// Deep-dive layer: 1 and 2 Chronicles. Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  12: [
    [2, `Why a history begins with lists`,
      `Chronicles opens with nine chapters of genealogies, written after the exile for a community that had lost its land and its kings. The Judah list (2:3-4:23) is the longest and moves through Tamar’s twins, Hezron’s sons, and Caleb’s families. It is full of odd details: Achan appears as “the troubler of Israel” (2:7), echoing Joshua 7, and Jabez gets a short prayer (4:9-10). A list does what narrative cannot: it says that the community still exists, connected back to Adam (1:1). In the Persian period, genealogy also established who could serve at the temple (Ezra 2:62). The Chronicler’s aim is continuity: the line from Adam to Judah to David shows that exile has not erased Israel.`,
      [[`Ezra 2:59-63`, `Priests who cannot prove their descent are excluded.`], [`Matthew 1:1-17`, `Matthew also opens with a genealogy that includes women and outsiders.`]],
      [`What does a genealogy say that a story cannot?`, `The Chronicler includes Tamar, Rahab and Ruth in David’s line. Why would an exile-era writer include them?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a three-generation genealogy of your own community, listing not only names but what each generation preserved.`]],
    [3, `David’s sons, in order`,
      `Chapter 3 lists David’s sons born in Hebron (six) and Jerusalem (thirteen, including Solomon and Nathan), then the line of Solomon down to the exile and beyond: Jehoiachin, his sons, and Zerubbabel (3:17-19). The list continues past the fall of Jerusalem and so is one of the latest passages in the Hebrew Bible, dated by the number of generations after Jehoiachin (perhaps to the late fifth century BCE). That the Davidic line is listed after the monarchy’s end signals a hope: the family still exists. Compare Haggai 2:23, where God calls Zerubbabel “my signet ring.” Bathsheba is called “Bath-shua, daughter of Ammiel” (3:5), a variant of 2 Samuel 11:3, and Solomon is the fourth of her sons, with no mention of Uriah.`,
      [[`Haggai 2:20-23`, `Zerubbabel as God’s signet ring.`], [`2 Kings 25:27-30`, `Jehoiachin released in Babylon.`]],
      [`The list goes past the exile. What does it say about the writer’s hope?`, `The Chronicler leaves out Bathsheba’s link to Uriah. What does his silence tell you about his aims?`],
      [`choose`, `Why list David’s line after the monarchy ended?`, `To keep a hope for a restored king alive.`, `To record a family that continued, regardless of political hope.`]],
    [4, `Jabez’s prayer in the middle of a list`,
      `In the middle of Judah’s genealogy, the writer stops for two verses: Jabez “was more honourable than his brothers,” his mother named him with pain (the Hebrew sounds like “pain”), and he prayed, “Oh that you would bless me and enlarge my border, and that your hand might be with me, and that you would keep me from harm so that it might not bring me pain!” God granted it (4:9-10). It is the only narrative in a long list, and its presence suggests that a name can be a destiny to be answered by prayer. The book popularised by Bruce Wilkinson (2000) turned the verse into a formula for blessing, which the text itself does not suggest. Whatever his story, his prayer is a simple request from someone with a name that predicts hurt.`,
      [[`Genesis 32:26-29`, `Jacob’s name is changed after he wrestles for a blessing.`], [`Ruth 4:11-12`, `A blessing on a family’s name.`]],
      [`What is it like to carry a name that was given in pain? What does Jabez do about it?`, `Why would a writer interrupt a list for a prayer?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a prayer in Jabez’s spirit about a name, label or history you have been given that you would like re-written.`]],
    [5, `Reuben, Gad and Manasseh, and the sin that took them away`,
      `The east-Jordan tribes are listed with a note that Reuben lost his birthright because he “defiled his father’s bed” (5:1; Genesis 35:22), and that it passed to Joseph’s sons. A paragraph on Gad, Reuben and Manasseh’s victory over the Hagrites includes a rare note about prayer: “they cried out to God in the battle, and he granted their urgent plea because they trusted in him” (5:20). The chapter ends with their exile: “the God of Israel stirred up the spirit of Pul king of Assyria… and he took them into exile” (5:26), because they “were unfaithful to the God of their fathers.” The Chronicler sees the exile as a consequence rooted in religious infidelity.`,
      [[`Genesis 49:3-4`, `Jacob’s oracle on Reuben.`], [`2 Kings 15:29`, `The Assyrian deportation of the east-Jordan region.`]],
      [`The Chronicler adds a note about prayer in battle. Why would he stress it?`, `A tribe that gains its victory by trust and loses its land by infidelity. What does the pattern suggest?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the Chronicler’s history teach?`, `That faithfulness leads to success and unfaithfulness to loss.`, `That history is shaped by many forces, and prayer and trust remain real in them.`]],
    [6, `The Levites and the music`,
      `Chapter 6 gives the Levitical genealogies and says that David appointed Levites “over the service of song in the house of the LORD” (6:31). Heman (Kohathite), Asaph (Gershonite) and Ethan (Merarite) head the three singing guilds. Psalm titles credit psalms to “the sons of Korah” (Heman’s clan), Asaph (Psalms 50, 73-83) and Ethan (Psalm 89). The list also gives Levitical cities (6:54-81), parallel to Joshua 21. Levites were not only sacrificial assistants; they were musicians and theologians. The Chronicler gives them unusual prominence: later in the book, Levites lead worship before a battle (2 Chronicles 20:21-22). Samuel, the prophet, appears in a Levitical line here (6:28), even though 1 Samuel calls him an Ephraimite (1:1).`,
      [[`Psalm 73:1-3`, `The first of the Asaph psalms.`], [`2 Chronicles 20:21-22`, `Singers go before the army.`]],
      [`The Chronicler makes musicians central to the temple. What does that say about his idea of worship?`, `Samuel is called an Ephraimite in one book and a Levite in another. How do you read the discrepancy?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the most likely purpose of the Levitical lists?`, `To establish the guilds’ authority in the second temple period.`, `To honour a tradition of worship that David had set in place.`]],
    [7, `Counting the northern tribes`,
      `Chapter 7 gives short lists for Issachar, Benjamin, Naphtali, Manasseh, Ephraim and Asher. Many of the entries are military: Issachar had 87,000 mighty men (7:5). Ephraim’s list includes the daughter Sheerah, “who built both Lower and Upper Beth-horon” (7:24), one of few women credited with building cities. The entry on Ephraim has a sad story: sons killed by Gath’s men while raiding cattle, and their father Ephraim “mourned many days” (7:21-22). Dan and Zebulun are missing, and the list for Naphtali is a single verse (7:13). The gaps may reflect the Chronicler’s sources and his focus on Judah, Levi and Benjamin, the tribes of the post-exilic community.`,
      [[`Genesis 46:8-27`, `The older list of Jacob’s family who went to Egypt.`], [`Numbers 26:5-51`, `The second wilderness census.`]],
      [`A woman is credited with founding cities in an otherwise male list. What do you do with that detail?`, `Dan and Zebulun are missing. What might explain omissions from the list?`],
      [`choose`, `Why would the Chronicler include northern tribes at all?`, `To insist that all Israel was part of the story.`, `To show that the community saw itself as heir to the whole nation.`]],
    [8, `Saul’s family is listed so it can fall`,
      `The Benjamin genealogy in chapter 8 ends with Saul’s line through Jonathan and Merib-baal (Mephibosheth), with a long list of descendants (8:33-40). The list is repeated in 9:35-44, in front of the story of Saul’s death in chapter 10. Saul’s son Ish-bosheth appears here as “Eshbaal” (8:33), the name 2 Samuel 2 gives as Ish-bosheth (“man of shame”), apparently a scribal change of *baal* to *bosheth*, “shame.” That kind of editing shows how the later text avoided the divine title Baal. The line preserved through Jonathan’s son, with 150 sons and grandsons (8:40), shows that Saul’s house continued even though the Chronicler’s narrative of his kingship is very short.`,
      [[`2 Samuel 2:8-10`, `Ish-bosheth, the “man of shame.”`], [`1 Chronicles 10:13-14`, `The Chronicler’s verdict on Saul.`]],
      [`“Baal” was a common word meaning “lord” and was also a divine name. Why might scribes change it in some names?`, `Saul’s family is listed twice. What does the Chronicler gain by doing so?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does the Chronicler list Saul’s line in detail?`, `To show the community in Benjamin, which was part of Judah’s post-exilic life.`, `To set up the contrast between Saul’s failure and David’s faithfulness.`]],
    [9, `Who came back to live in Jerusalem`,
      `Chapter 9 moves from the exile to the returnees: Israelites, priests, Levites and temple servants, and lists the Levite gatekeepers and the roles of those who guard the thresholds, bake the offering bread and sing night and day (9:17-33). The chapter parallels Nehemiah 11, a list of those who settled in Jerusalem after the return, with some differences. The details are practical: some kept the keys of the temple chambers, some oversaw the vessels, some mixed the spices. The writer shows that sacred community depends on thousands of small, mundane tasks done reliably. The final lines repeat Saul’s genealogy as a bridge to his death.`,
      [[`Nehemiah 11:1-21`, `The parallel list of those who settled in Jerusalem.`], [`Psalm 84:10`, `“I would rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God.”`]],
      [`The list includes bakers and key-keepers. What does it say about the value of unglamorous service?`, `Why would an exiled community want a record of who lived where?`],
      [`rate`, `How much do you value behind-the-scenes work in your community?`, `Little`, `A great deal`]],
    [10, `Saul’s death in one verdict`,
      `The Chronicler’s version of Saul’s death (1 Samuel 31) is compressed and followed by a theological summary: “So Saul died for his breach of faith. He broke faith with the LORD in that he did not keep the command of the LORD, and also consulted a medium, seeking guidance. He did not seek guidance from the LORD. Therefore the LORD put him to death and turned the kingdom over to David” (10:13-14). The editorial statement reads Saul’s reign as a single moral lesson. Chronicles leaves out the long story of Saul and David’s rivalry and begins David’s story with his coronation at Hebron. The effect is to present David as the chosen king without a messy transition.`,
      [[`1 Samuel 28:3-25`, `Saul and the medium at Endor.`], [`1 Samuel 31:1-13`, `The original account of Saul’s death.`]],
      [`The Chronicler turns Saul’s reign into one sentence of judgment. What is lost and what is gained?`, `Compare the Chronicler’s Saul with the Saul of 1 Samuel. Which is more human?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the omission of the rivalry between Saul and David do?`, `It smooths David’s image and focuses on temple and worship.`, `It reflects a different aim: not a history of events but a theology of kingship.`]],
    [11, `David’s mighty men, and a promise by all Israel`,
      `After Saul’s death, “all Israel” gather at Hebron to make David king, and he takes Jerusalem with Joab leading the attack (11:1-9). The Chronicler then lists David’s mighty men, parallel to 2 Samuel 23, including the story of the three who broke through to bring water from the well at Bethlehem (11:15-19). In the Chronicler’s telling, the support for David is immediate and unified, contrasted with 2 Samuel’s seven years of war between Judah and the house of Saul. The text uses “all Israel” more than forty times in the book. David’s kingship is presented as a gift of the whole nation, an ideal for a post-exilic community that was divided and small.`,
      [[`2 Samuel 23:8-39`, `The parallel list of the Thirty.`], [`2 Samuel 5:1-3`, `The parallel account of David’s anointing.`]],
      [`The Chronicler says “all Israel” made David king. What is the effect of that language?`, `Why might a post-exilic writer dwell on national unity?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the Chronicler’s smoother story?`, `As a theological ideal that skips the conflict.`, `As a different tradition that emphasises the later consensus.`]],
    [12, `The men who came to David`,
      `Chapter 12 lists those who joined David at Ziklag and Hebron, tribe by tribe. The Benjaminites could “shoot arrows and sling stones with either the right or the left hand” (12:2), the Gadites were “mighty men… whose faces were like the faces of lions” (12:8), and the men of Issachar “had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do” (12:32). A famous line has Amasai say, “We are yours, O David, and with you, O son of Jesse! Peace, peace to you” (12:18). The chapter closes with a feast of three days at Hebron and “there was joy in Israel” (12:40). The list shows the range of gifts a community needs, including the discernment of Issachar.`,
      [[`Esther 4:14`, `“Who knows whether you have not come to the kingdom for such a time as this?”`], [`Matthew 16:3`, `Jesus asks his hearers to read the signs of the times.`]],
      [`“Understanding of the times.” What does it take to read your own times well?`, `The list gathers fighters, discerners and a unified crowd. Which role do you most often play?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write the one-sentence description a historian would give of your community’s gift to its moment.`]],
    [13, `Two tries to bring the ark`,
      `The Chronicler’s account of the ark’s journey is longer than Samuel’s. David consults all the leaders (13:1-4) and the first attempt fails with Uzzah’s death. After the second, successful move, David wears a linen robe and dances, as in 2 Samuel, and Michal despises him (15:29). The Chronicler adds a reflection in 15:13: “Because you did not carry it the first time, the LORD our God broke out against us, because we did not seek him according to the rule.” The first attempt used a cart, which was the Philistine practice (1 Samuel 6:7). The second uses Levites with poles, as the law says (Exodus 25:14; Numbers 4:15). The ark is also placed in a tent, with Obed-edom’s household blessed.`,
      [[`Numbers 4:15`, `The law that the Kohathites carry holy things, not touching them.`], [`1 Samuel 6:7-12`, `The Philistines’ new cart, the pattern David first imitates.`]],
      [`David first copies the Philistines’ method and then repents. What does the story say about borrowing practices?`, `The Chronicler explains Uzzah’s death as a failure to seek God “according to the rule.” Is that explanation satisfying?`],
      [`choose`, `What should we make of the cart?`, `A good intention with the wrong method.`, `A sign of a ruler more used to imitating neighbours than following the law.`]],
    [16, `David’s psalm of thanks, assembled from three psalms`,
      `When the ark reaches Jerusalem, David appoints Asaph and his kin to give thanks (16:7), and the psalm in 16:8-36 combines Psalm 105:1-15, Psalm 96:1-13 and Psalm 106:1, 47-48. A single psalm in Chronicles is a medley, which shows the writer working with an existing Psalter and re-arranging it to fit the occasion. The mix of themes is deliberate: the God who made a covenant (Psalm 105), the God who is king over all nations (Psalm 96) and the plea to gather the scattered (Psalm 106) speak directly to a post-exilic audience. The ark settles in Jerusalem, with daily offerings, singers and gatekeepers, and the structure of regular worship begins.`,
      [[`Psalm 96:1-13`, `The song of the earth’s renewal that the Chronicler places in David’s mouth.`], [`Psalm 106:47-48`, `The plea “gather us from among the nations.”`]],
      [`The Chronicler built David’s psalm from three earlier psalms. What does that say about how scripture develops?`, `Why include a plea for gathering from the nations in a story of the ark’s arrival?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the medley?`, `As a creative use of the Psalter for an exiled community.`, `As a later liturgical composition attributed to David.`]],
    [17, `A promise with changed wording`,
      `The Chronicler retells the Davidic covenant (17:1-15) almost word for word from 2 Samuel 7, with telling changes: where Samuel has “your house and your kingdom shall be made sure forever before me,” Chronicles has “I will confirm him in my house and in my kingdom forever” (17:14), so that the throne is God’s own. The clause about disciplining the king for iniquity (2 Samuel 7:14) is omitted. Chapter 18 then lists David’s wars in brief. The changes reinforce a point: for the Chronicler, the Davidic throne is “the throne of the kingdom of the LORD” (28:5), and its permanence is not in doubt, though the post-exilic reality is different. Hope is transferred from a king to a worshipping community.`,
      [[`2 Samuel 7:12-16`, `The original promise.`], [`1 Chronicles 28:5`, `Solomon sits “on the throne of the kingdom of the LORD over Israel.”`]],
      [`The Chronicler removes the clause about discipline. What do you think he is doing?`, `If God owns the throne, what changes when no king sits on it?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the changed wording suggest?`, `That the Chronicler emphasises God’s kingship over human failure.`, `That he idealises David by omitting the warnings.`]],
    [19, `A war the Chronicler does not skip, with a story he does`,
      `The Ammonite war (19:1-20:3) parallels 2 Samuel 10-12 but leaves out the Bathsheba and Uriah story entirely, jumping from Joab’s siege of Rabbah to David taking the crown from its king. The omission is one of the most discussed features of Chronicles. The Chronicler also leaves out Absalom’s rebellion, Amnon and Tamar, and Adonijah’s coup. David’s portrait is deliberately idealised. A later chapter (21) does include David’s census sin, perhaps because it leads to the purchase of the temple site. Readers who know Samuel and Chronicles together have two portraits of David: the human king and the founder of worship.`,
      [[`2 Samuel 11:1-12:25`, `The story of Bathsheba that Chronicles omits.`], [`1 Chronicles 21:1-22:1`, `The census, which Chronicles keeps because it ends at the temple’s site.`]],
      [`Why would the Chronicler omit David’s worst sin? Is it dishonest, or is it a different kind of history?`, `What would you lose if you read only one of the two portraits?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the silences in Chronicles?`, `As deliberate theological selection for a community that needed hope.`, `As a gap that leaves the picture incomplete, to be filled by Samuel.`]],
    [22, `David prepares what he may not build`,
      `David is told that he cannot build the temple because he “shed much blood” (22:8), a detail absent from Samuel. He gathers cedar, iron and gold, supposedly 100,000 talents of gold and 1,000,000 talents of silver (22:14), a number that vastly exceeds the world’s supply in antiquity and is usually taken as hyperbole or a scribal inflation. Solomon is called “a man of rest” (22:9), and God promises peace in his days. The chapter shows an old king preparing for a project that others will finish. Paul would use a similar image in 1 Corinthians 3:10-15, building on a foundation others laid.`,
      [[`1 Kings 5:3-5`, `Solomon’s version: David was too busy with wars.`], [`1 Corinthians 3:10-15`, `Paul’s image of building on a foundation.`]],
      [`David is barred from building because of bloodshed. What does that say about the relation between violence and holiness?`, `How would you prepare for a project you know you will not finish?`],
      [`rate`, `How do you feel about work whose results you will not see?`, `Unsatisfying`, `Meaningful`]],
    [23, `Levites reorganised from age twenty`,
      `David numbers the Levites at thirty and over (38,000), then re-assigns them: 24,000 oversee the temple work, 6,000 are officers and judges, 4,000 gatekeepers, and 4,000 musicians (23:3-5). He lowers the entry age to twenty because the temple will no longer be moved (23:24-26). The shift in age is one of the clearest examples of Chronicles adapting earlier law to a new situation: Numbers 4 had set thirty, and Numbers 8:24 twenty-five. The statement “the Levites no longer need to carry the tabernacle” gives a rationale. Law in this view is purposeful: the number changes when the work changes. The musicians are said to “thank and praise the LORD” morning and evening (23:30).`,
      [[`Numbers 4:3`, `Service from age thirty.`], [`Numbers 8:24-26`, `Service from age twenty-five and retirement at fifty.`]],
      [`David lowers the age because the work has changed. What principle of interpretation is being modelled?`, `Why would a temple need musicians who “thank and praise” every morning?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the three ages (thirty, twenty-five, twenty)?`, `As an adaptation of law to changing needs.`, `As evidence of different traditions that the Chronicler harmonised.`]],
    [24, `Twenty-four priestly divisions chosen by lot`,
      `David and the priests Zadok and Ahimelech divide Aaron’s descendants into twenty-four courses by lot, sixteen from Eleazar and eight from Ithamar, each serving for a week at a time (24:1-19). The lot is “before the king… and the chiefs” and “no one was favoured.” Luke 1:5 places Zechariah in “the division of Abijah,” the eighth course (24:10), which fixes his service in the temple when Gabriel appears. The Dead Sea Scrolls (4QMishmarot) preserve a calendar of these courses, and after the destruction of the temple the lists of the courses were remembered in synagogue poems, which later helped researchers trace where priestly families had settled in Galilee.`,
      [[`Luke 1:5-9`, `Zechariah served in the course of Abijah.`], [`Nehemiah 12:1-7`, `Priests who returned with Zerubbabel, listed by name.`]],
      [`Casting lots made the assignments seem impartial. What do you gain and lose by deciding on assignments this way?`, `How does a list of priestly courses help to read Luke 1?`],
      [`choose`, `Why preserve these lists?`, `They anchor temple service in David’s order.`, `They show how an institution keeps its place across centuries.`]],
    [25, `Musicians who “prophesy with lyres”`,
      `David sets apart Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun’s sons “for the service of prophesying with lyres, with harps, and with cymbals” (25:1). The word “prophesy” is applied to musicians, linking song and inspiration. Heman is called “the king’s seer” (25:5). The 288 trained singers are divided into twenty-four groups by lot, matching the priestly courses (25:7-31). The chapter shows an ancient tradition in which music is an expression of prophecy and teaching, not mere accompaniment. The same sense appears in 2 Kings 3:15, where Elisha asks for a musician before he prophesies, and in 1 Samuel 10:5 where prophets come down playing instruments.`,
      [[`2 Kings 3:15`, `Elisha asks for a musician.`], [`Psalm 150:1-6`, `The last psalm, calling for instruments of every kind.`]],
      [`What does it mean to “prophesy with a lyre”? What is music doing that speech cannot?`, `The singers are trained and numbered. What does that say about the value of skill in worship?`],
      [`rate`, `How much do you rely on music in your spiritual life?`, `Little`, `A great deal`]],
    [26, `Gatekeepers and treasurers`,
      `Chapter 26 gives lists of gatekeepers, including Obed-edom’s family, whose blessing from the ark is repeated (26:4-5), and treasurers “over the treasuries of the house of God and… the dedicated gifts” (26:20-28). Among the gifts were spoils from war that David and others dedicated to maintain the house of the LORD (26:26-27). The detail shows a worship system with financial oversight. Some Levites served as officers and judges “for the outward work of God’s house” (26:29), and some were over “all the work of the LORD and for the service of the king” in the territory west of the Jordan (26:30). The sacred and civil administration overlapped in this vision of the kingdom.`,
      [[`2 Kings 12:4-16`, `The record of temple funds and the repairs under Joash.`], [`Nehemiah 13:10-13`, `Nehemiah appoints treasurers to restore the Levites’ shares.`]],
      [`The chapter presents financial oversight as part of worship. Why would an ancient writer care?`, `What would it mean to dedicate the “spoils of war” to the upkeep of sacred space?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the overlap of sacred and civil administration?`, `As a sign that all of life was seen as under God’s rule.`, `As a feature of a monarchy that used the temple for political legitimacy.`]],
    [27, `A king’s year divided into twelve months`,
      `Chapter 27 lists twelve army divisions of 24,000 men, each serving a month in rotation (27:1-15), tribal leaders, the king’s stewards and his counsellors. Ahithophel and Hushai appear among the counsellors (27:33), and “Joab was the commander of the king’s army.” The rotation system means a total standing force of 288,000, a number that strains credibility for a small nation, and it may represent an ideal or a large militia. David’s overseers were responsible for storehouses, vineyards, olive trees, herds and flocks. The picture is of a kingdom organised like a household. After the story of the census in chapter 21, the omission of numbers for Gad and Asher (27:23) is noted, “because the LORD had promised to make Israel as numerous as the stars.”`,
      [[`2 Samuel 24:1-9`, `The census, with a parallel count.`], [`Genesis 15:5`, `The promise of descendants like the stars.`]],
      [`The Chronicler says David did not count those under twenty because of God’s promise (27:23). Why include that?`, `Is a kingdom run like a household a good or dangerous model?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the large numbers?`, `As literal counts of a large mobilisation.`, `As an idealised portrait of a kingdom well ordered.`]],
    [28, `David’s last address and the plans for the temple`,
      `David summons the officials of Israel and announces that God has chosen Solomon to build the temple (28:1-10). He hands Solomon “the plan of the vestibule… of the house… of the Holy of Holies” and says, “All this he made clear to me in writing from the hand of the LORD, all the work to be done according to the plan” (28:11-19). The line implies a divine blueprint, as Moses received for the tabernacle (Exodus 25:9, 40). His charge to Solomon: “Be strong and do it. Do not be afraid or dismayed” (28:20), and “If you seek him, he will be found by you; but if you forsake him, he will cast you off forever” (28:9). The speech sets the conditions for later kings: faithfulness secures the house.`,
      [[`Exodus 25:40`, `The pattern shown to Moses for the tabernacle.`], [`Hebrews 8:5`, `The writer says earthly priests serve a copy of the heavenly pattern.`]],
      [`David says the plan was given “in writing from the hand of the LORD.” What does it mean to receive a plan you did not design?`, `“If you seek him, he will be found.” How does the promise shape the Chronicler’s history of the kings?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading 2 Chronicles, predict which kings the Chronicler will praise and why, based on this charge to Solomon.`]]
  ],
  13: [
    [1, `Solomon’s dream at Gibeon, and a different emphasis`,
      `The Chronicler narrates Solomon going to the tabernacle at Gibeon with a thousand burnt offerings and God appearing in a dream (1:3-13). Solomon asks for wisdom “to go out and come in before this people” (1:10), and God adds riches and honour. The chapter also highlights that all Israel, “the whole assembly,” goes with him. After the dream the Chronicler adds a note on Solomon’s horses and chariots and trade in Egypt (1:14-17). The Chronicler passes over the troubled succession, the killings of Adonijah and Joab, and Solomon’s early foreign marriage described in 1 Kings 3:1. His Solomon begins in worship, emphasising the legitimacy of the temple project.`,
      [[`1 Kings 3:4-15`, `The parallel account of the dream.`], [`James 1:5`, `“If any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask God.”`]],
      [`Solomon asks for wisdom to lead. What would you ask for if you were given one request for your work?`, `The Chronicler skips the violent opening of Solomon’s reign. What is lost?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own “request at Gibeon” in three sentences: what you would ask for and why.`]],
    [3, `The temple’s measurements, and names for the pillars`,
      `The Chronicler’s temple chapters (3-4) repeat 1 Kings 6-7 with changes: the building starts on Mount Moriah, “where the LORD had appeared to David… on the threshing floor of Ornan the Jebusite” (3:1), linking the site to Abraham’s Moriah (Genesis 22:2). The two bronze pillars are given as 35 cubits tall here, against 18 in 1 Kings 7:15, which may reflect a different measure or a combined height. They are named Jachin (“he establishes”) and Boaz (“in him is strength”) (3:17). The cherubim in the inner sanctuary have wings spanning twenty cubits. The link with Genesis 22 suggests a place where God provides.`,
      [[`Genesis 22:1-14`, `Abraham on Moriah: “the LORD will provide.”`], [`1 Kings 7:15-22`, `The pillars with a different height.`]],
      [`Linking the temple site with Abraham’s Moriah adds a layer. What does it say about the site’s meaning?`, `Two accounts give different pillar heights. How do you treat details that do not agree?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you handle the numerical differences between Kings and Chronicles?`, `As scribal variation or different measurement systems.`, `As evidence that the Chronicler used different sources for his own purposes.`]],
    [5, `The ark enters and the glory fills the house`,
      `At the temple dedication, priests carry the ark into the Holy of Holies, and the Levitical singers, in white linen with cymbals, harps and lyres, with 120 priests blowing trumpets, make “one voice” in praise: “For he is good, for his steadfast love endures forever” (5:12-13). Then a cloud fills the house so that the priests “could not stand to minister” (5:13-14). The scene recalls Exodus 40:34-35, when the cloud filled the tabernacle. The Chronicler stresses musical unity. The refrain “his steadfast love endures forever” (*ki le’olam hasdo*) is the central line of Psalm 136, and it is repeated in the Chronicler’s accounts of worship (1 Chronicles 16:34; 2 Chronicles 7:3, 6; 20:21). It becomes the community’s creed.`,
      [[`Exodus 40:34-35`, `The cloud fills the tabernacle, and Moses cannot enter.`], [`Psalm 136:1-26`, `The psalm built around the same refrain.`]],
      [`The priests cannot stand to minister because of the cloud. What does that say about control in worship?`, `A refrain repeated in worship becomes a confession. What refrain carries your community?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the central point of the scene?`, `God’s presence overwhelms human arrangements.`, `Unity in praise is the occasion of God’s presence.`]],
    [6, `Solomon’s prayer for foreigners and exiles`,
      `Solomon’s dedication prayer (6:12-42) lists seven situations: oaths, defeat, drought, famine, a foreigner’s prayer (6:32-33), war, and exile. The foreigner request is notable: “Do according to all for which the foreigner calls to you, in order that all the peoples of the earth may know your name.” The prayer for exile asks God to hear “if they turn back to you with all their heart… in the land of their captivity” (6:36-39), which would have had real force for the Chronicler’s readers. The prayer ends with a quote from Psalm 132:8-10, a rare moment of the Chronicler quoting the Psalter. The temple is imagined as a house of prayer, which Isaiah 56:7 calls “a house of prayer for all peoples.”`,
      [[`Isaiah 56:6-7`, `Foreigners who join themselves to the LORD.`], [`Daniel 6:10`, `Daniel prays toward Jerusalem from exile.`]],
      [`Solomon prays about foreigners and exiles. Why would the Chronicler highlight those two?`, `What does it mean to pray “toward” a place you cannot reach?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own “seven situations” prayer for your community: seven crises you would ask God to hear.`]],
    [9, `The queen of Sheba, retold`,
      `The Chronicler retells the Sheba visit (9:1-12) and Solomon’s wealth (9:13-28) with small changes from 1 Kings 10. He adds a note that Solomon “made silver as common as stones in Jerusalem” (9:27) and that his gold came every three years in ships to Tarshish (9:21). Chapter 9 ends with Solomon’s death, and the Chronicler has left out the account of his foreign wives and the idolatry in 1 Kings 11. In the book of Kings, Solomon’s failure leads to the division. In Chronicles, the cause is Rehoboam’s harshness, not Solomon’s sin. The choice shows a different way of explaining history.`,
      [[`1 Kings 11:1-13`, `Solomon’s turning away, which Chronicles omits.`], [`Ecclesiastes 2:4-11`, `The Preacher, traditionally Solomon, on wealth and its emptiness.`]],
      [`Chronicles omits the idolatry of Solomon’s old age. What effect does that have on the portrait?`, `How might Ecclesiastes be read as an answer to the glory in this chapter?`],
      [`choose`, `Why would the Chronicler leave out Solomon’s sin?`, `To focus on the temple builder and the ideal reign.`, `To avoid blaming the dynasty and its legitimacy.`]],
    [10, `The tribes secede`,
      `Rehoboam goes to Shechem to be made king. Jeroboam and the people ask him to lighten the “heavy yoke.” Older advisers counsel a gentle answer; the young men say, “My little finger is thicker than my father’s thighs… my father disciplined you with whips, but I will discipline you with scorpions” (10:10-11). The northern tribes answer, “What portion do we have in David?” (10:16) and stone the taskmaster Adoram. In Chronicles the story ends with Jeroboam’s kingdom, and the Chronicler says the division was “a turn of affairs from God” (10:15). Rehoboam’s mistake is a classic failure of leadership: he listens to those who tell him what he wants to hear.`,
      [[`Proverbs 15:1`, `“A soft answer turns away wrath.”`], [`2 Samuel 20:1`, `Sheba’s slogan, the same cry.`]],
      [`Rehoboam ignored the elders and followed the young. What does the story say about counsel?`, `The narrator says that God’s purpose was behind the split. How does that interact with Rehoboam’s choice?`],
      [`rate`, `How easy is it for you to hear advice that tells you to give up power?`, `Hard`, `Easy`]],
    [12, `Abijah’s speech from a mountain`,
      `Abijah of Judah, Rehoboam’s son, stands on Mount Zemaraim and makes a speech to Jeroboam’s army before battle, accusing the north of using golden calves and non-Levitical priests while Judah keeps the proper worship (13:4-12). “God is with us at our head, and his priests with their battle trumpets” (13:12). Despite being outflanked, Judah’s men cry out, the priests blow the trumpets, and God defeats the Israelites, killing 500,000 (13:17), a number likely stylised. The story is found only in Chronicles, and the king’s speech reflects the Chronicler’s concerns about correct worship. Kings gives Abijam a poor evaluation, “his heart was not wholly true” (1 Kings 15:3).`,
      [[`1 Kings 15:1-8`, `The brief, negative account of Abijam in Kings.`], [`1 Kings 12:28-31`, `Jeroboam’s golden calves and non-Levite priests.`]],
      [`Kings and Chronicles judge Abijah differently. How do you handle two verdicts?`, `The battle is won by prayer and trumpet, not strategy. What do you make of the argument that right worship brings victory?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the difference between Kings and Chronicles on Abijah?`, `The Chronicler adds a source about a positive moment in a mixed reign.`, `Each author uses the king to illustrate his own theology.`]],
    [14, `Asa’s prayer when outnumbered`,
      `Zerah the Cushite comes against Asa with a million men and 300 chariots (14:9). Asa prays, “O LORD, there is none like you to help, between the mighty and the weak” (14:11). God defeats the Cushites and the Judahites chase them to Gerar. Then the prophet Azariah tells Asa, “The LORD is with you while you are with him. If you seek him, he will be found by you” (15:2), and Asa leads a reform and covenant. The Chronicler uses the moment to illustrate his “seek” theology, in which seeking God is a condition for blessing. The number of the Cushite army is likely a conventional large number, and its identification with Osorkon I of Egypt has been suggested.`,
      [[`2 Chronicles 15:1-15`, `Azariah’s prophecy and the covenant renewal.`], [`1 Kings 15:9-24`, `The shorter account of Asa in Kings.`]],
      [`Asa’s prayer admits his weakness before an overwhelming army. When have you prayed from that position?`, `The Chronicler’s theology is “seek and be found.” What are its strengths and limits?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the prayer?`, `As a model of reliance in the face of overwhelming odds.`, `As a literary device to illustrate the writer’s theme of seeking God.`]],
    [16, `Asa’s decline: he trusts a doctor and a treaty`,
      `In his thirty-sixth year, Asa buys the help of Ben-hadad of Syria against Baasha. The seer Hanani rebukes him: “Because you relied on the king of Syria, and did not rely on the LORD your God… For the eyes of the LORD run to and fro throughout the whole earth, to give strong support to those whose heart is blameless toward him” (16:7-9). Asa puts Hanani in the stocks. In his last years Asa is diseased in his feet: “yet even in his disease he did not seek the LORD, but sought help from the physicians” (16:12). The Chronicler is not condemning medicine so much as the failure to seek God while using it. Asa’s funeral had a great fire of spices (16:14).`,
      [[`1 Kings 15:16-22`, `The parallel account of Asa’s treaty with Ben-hadad.`], [`Sirach 38:1-15`, `A later text that honours physicians as God’s gift.`]],
      [`The Chronicler faults Asa for seeking physicians but not God. How would you apply that without rejecting medicine?`, `Hanani is imprisoned for telling the truth. How do leaders respond to a prophet who criticises them?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read verse 12?`, `As a condemnation of seeking only human help.`, `As a critique of Asa’s attitude, not of the physicians.`]],
    [18, `Micaiah again, from Judah’s side`,
      `Jehoshaphat, a good king, makes a marriage alliance with Ahab (18:1) and joins his campaign to Ramoth-gilead. The story of Micaiah and the four hundred prophets (18:4-27) parallels 1 Kings 22. In the following chapter, the seer Jehu son of Hanani meets Jehoshaphat on his return: “Should you help the wicked and love those who hate the LORD? Because of this, wrath has come upon you from the LORD” (19:2). The Chronicler likes to show a king rebuked by a prophet and then repenting. Jehoshaphat then sets up judges throughout the land and tells them, “Consider what you do, for you judge not for man but for the LORD” (19:6).`,
      [[`1 Kings 22:1-40`, `The original Micaiah story.`], [`Deuteronomy 16:18-20`, `The law on appointing judges and the demand for justice.`]],
      [`Jehoshaphat was right to listen to Micaiah and wrong to join Ahab. What does that say about a good king in a bad alliance?`, `Why does the Chronicler give a speech to judges after the rebuke?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the Chronicler’s lesson about alliances?`, `That partnership with the wicked brings wrath even for the righteous.`, `That reform can follow a failure, as Jehoshaphat’s judicial reform did.`]],
    [21, `Jehoram’s murders and Elijah’s letter`,
      `Jehoram of Judah, married to Athaliah, kills all his brothers and some officials to secure the throne (21:4). The Chronicler records a letter from Elijah: “Because you have not walked in the ways of Jehoshaphat… behold, the LORD will bring a great plague on your people” (21:12-15). It is the only mention of a written prophetic letter from Elijah, and it is odd because Elijah had probably been taken up before Jehoram’s reign, according to the chronology in Kings. The king dies painfully of a disease of the bowels, and “no one desired his death” and no fires were made for him (21:19-20). The judgement comes through a combination of political and bodily collapse.`,
      [[`2 Kings 8:16-24`, `The shorter account of Jehoram in Kings.`], [`2 Kings 2:1-12`, `Elijah’s departure, which fits oddly with this letter.`]],
      [`Elijah’s letter is unique. How do you treat a text that seems to conflict with the chronology in Kings?`, `The king’s death is met with silence. What does a funeral without mourning say?`],
      [`choose`, `How might we explain the letter?`, `Elijah wrote it before his departure as a prophecy.`, `The Chronicler uses a literary device to give prophetic weight to Jehoram’s failure.`]],
    [23, `The coup against Athaliah, retold with Levites`,
      `The Chronicler retells the coup of 2 Kings 11 with a change: Jehoiada brings in the Levites and the heads of families from the cities of Judah, and the guards at the temple doors are Levites and priests, not the foreign royal bodyguards (23:2-7). Only those who are holy may enter the temple, so the Chronicler adjusts the story to respect the temple’s purity. The rest of the story is the same: Joash is crowned and Athaliah is killed outside the temple precincts (23:14-15). The altered detail shows how the Chronicler’s priorities shape his rewriting: laws about holiness could not be violated even in a coup. Jehoiada makes a covenant between himself, the people and the king that they should be the LORD’s people.`,
      [[`2 Kings 11:4-12`, `The original version with the Carites and guards.`], [`Numbers 18:1-7`, `The law that restricts temple access.`]],
      [`The Chronicler rewrites the guard to fit the temple laws. What does that say about his method?`, `Jehoiada makes a covenant between God, king and people. What does the three-way structure suggest?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you evaluate the Chronicler’s changes?`, `As a legitimate interpretive rewriting of an older story.`, `As a harmonisation that removes historical detail.`]],
    [24, `Joash after Jehoiada: a murder in the temple court`,
      `Joash does what is right “all the days of Jehoiada” (24:2). He repairs the temple using the chest for offerings, and after Jehoiada dies at 130, the officials persuade him to abandon the temple and worship Asherim and idols (24:17-18). Jehoiada’s son Zechariah the priest warns them: “Why do you break the commandments of the LORD, so that you cannot prosper?” and the people stone him in the court, at the king’s command. As he dies he says, “May the LORD see and avenge!” (24:20-22). The Arameans invade, and Joash is murdered in his bed by his servants. Jesus’ words in Matthew 23:35 about the blood of Zechariah, “whom you murdered between the sanctuary and the altar,” may refer to this event, since 2 Chronicles is the last book in the Hebrew order.`,
      [[`Matthew 23:34-36`, `Jesus’ reference to the killing of Zechariah.`], [`2 Kings 12:17-21`, `The shorter account of Joash’s decline and death.`]],
      [`Joash is faithful as long as his mentor lives. What does that say about borrowed faith?`, `Zechariah is murdered for speaking the truth. How does Jesus’ reference extend the story?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of Joash’s change?`, `A king whose loyalty depended on his adviser, not his heart.`, `A story that shows how corrupt officials can capture a young king.`]],
    [25, `Amaziah hires mercenaries and then changes his mind`,
      `Amaziah hires 100,000 mercenaries from Israel for 100 talents of silver. A man of God tells him to send them home, because the LORD is not with Israel. “But what shall we do about the hundred talents?” Amaziah asks, and the man answers, “The LORD is able to give you much more than this” (25:9). The mercenaries, angry, raid Judah’s towns on their way back. After defeating Edom, Amaziah brings home the Edomite gods and worships them, which leads to the defeat by Joash of Israel (25:14-24). The sequence shows the cost of both loyalty and compromise. The question “what shall we do about the hundred talents?” is one of the most practical in the Bible: what do you do about a sunk cost when obedience requires loss?`,
      [[`Matthew 6:19-21`, `Where your treasure is, there your heart will be.`], [`2 Kings 14:8-14`, `The parallel account of the war with Israel.`]],
      [`“What shall we do about the hundred talents?” What is the modern version of this question in your life?`, `Amaziah obeys on the mercenaries and then worships the gods of the people he defeated. What does that say about success?`],
      [`rate`, `How easily do you let go of a lost investment when you know it’s right to do so?`, `Hard`, `Easy`]],
    [26, `Uzziah’s strength and his pride`,
      `Uzziah (Azariah) reigns fifty-two years, builds towers, digs cisterns, develops agriculture, and organises a large army with “engines… to shoot arrows and great stones” (26:15). “His fame spread far, for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong. But when he was strong, he grew proud, to his destruction” (26:15-16). He enters the temple to burn incense, though only priests may, and leprosy breaks out on his forehead as the priests confront him. He lives in a separate house until his death (26:19-21). Isaiah’s call vision takes place “in the year that King Uzziah died” (Isaiah 6:1). An archaeological find, a burial inscription from the Second Temple period, says “here were brought the bones of Uzziah king of Judah, do not open.”`,
      [[`Isaiah 6:1-5`, `Isaiah’s vision in the year of Uzziah’s death.`], [`Numbers 16:1-35`, `Korah’s incense, the earlier case of non-priests offering at the altar.`]],
      [`The Chronicler says “when he was strong, he grew proud.” How often is strength the setting for failure?`, `Isaiah’s vision of a holy God comes in the year the proud king dies. What does the timing suggest?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Uzziah’s sin?`, `A king’s attempt to claim priestly authority.`, `A general fall that follows prosperity.`]],
    [28, `Ahaz, and the Israelites who sent captives home`,
      `Ahaz sacrifices in the high places, burns his sons, and loses to Aram and Israel. The northern army of Pekah takes 200,000 captives from Judah, and the prophet Oded meets them at Samaria: “Behold, because the LORD, the God of your fathers, was angry with Judah, he gave them into your hand, but you have slaughtered them in a rage that has reached up to heaven. And now you intend to subjugate the people of Judah… Have you not sins of your own against the LORD your God?” (28:9-10). Leaders of Ephraim then clothe the captives from the spoil, give them sandals, food and drink, anoint their wounds, put the weak on donkeys, and take them to Jericho (28:15). Jesus’ parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:30-37), set on the road to Jericho, may echo this story.`,
      [[`Luke 10:25-37`, `The parable of the Good Samaritan, on the Jericho road.`], [`Isaiah 7:1-9`, `Isaiah’s warning to Ahaz in the same war.`]],
      [`The Israelites, enemies of Judah, treat the captives with compassion. What made them listen to Oded?`, `A parallel with the Good Samaritan: does the echo change how you read either story?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the most striking feature of the story?`, `A northern prophet persuades his own army to act with mercy.`, `A reversal in which the enemy shows the compassion that the king lacked.`]],
    [29, `Hezekiah reopens the doors`,
      `Hezekiah’s first act in the first month of his first year is to reopen the doors of the temple, which Ahaz had shut, and repair them (29:3). He gathers the priests and Levites and says, “Our fathers have been unfaithful… Now it is in my heart to make a covenant with the LORD” (29:5-10). They need sixteen days to cleanse the temple, and then offer a sin offering for the kingdom, the sanctuary and Judah (29:20-24). The Levites are more upright in heart than the priests in sanctifying themselves (29:34). Singing, with David’s psalms and Asaph’s, accompanies the burnt offering. The Chronicler’s Hezekiah is almost a second Solomon, a restorer of worship, with more space given to the reform than to the Assyrian invasion.`,
      [[`2 Kings 18:1-8`, `The shorter account of Hezekiah’s reform.`], [`Psalm 24:7-10`, `A psalm of doors and gates, fitting for the reopening.`]],
      [`Hezekiah’s first act is to repair what his father closed. What does it mean to begin by restoring?`, `The Levites were more diligent than the priests. What does the text imply about leadership in religious institutions?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write the first act you would take on day one of restoring a damaged community, and why it would come first.`]],
    [30, `A Passover for a divided people`,
      `Hezekiah sends couriers throughout Israel and Judah, even to the northern tribes, inviting them to Jerusalem for the Passover. Many scorn the invitation, but some from Asher, Manasseh and Zebulun humble themselves and come (30:10-11). Because the priests were not ready, the Passover was held in the second month, as permitted by Numbers 9:10-11. Many had not purified themselves, and Hezekiah prays, “May the good LORD pardon everyone who sets his heart to seek God… though not according to the sanctuary’s rules of cleanness” (30:18-19). “And the LORD heard Hezekiah and healed the people.” The feast lasts fourteen days, and “there was great joy in Jerusalem, for since the time of Solomon… there had been nothing like this” (30:26).`,
      [[`Numbers 9:6-13`, `The provision for a second Passover.`], [`Mark 2:23-28`, `Jesus: the Sabbath was made for man, a principle similar to this mercy.`]],
      [`Hezekiah prays for those who were not properly purified but sought God sincerely. What does that say about intent and rule?`, `Some northerners scorned the invitation and some came. What decides who comes?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Hezekiah’s prayer?`, `A principle of mercy for sincere hearts over ritual correctness.`, `A pragmatic accommodation to a partly prepared people.`]],
    [31, `The tithes pile up in heaps`,
      `After the Passover, the people destroy the pillars and Asherim and bring the tithes of grain, wine, oil and honey so generously that they lay them “in heaps” for four months (31:6-7). Azariah the chief priest says, “Since the people began to bring the contributions into the house of the LORD, we have had enough to eat and have plenty left, for the LORD has blessed his people” (31:10). Storehouses are prepared, and the Levites are given their shares, including their children (31:16-19). The Chronicler closes the chapter with a summary of Hezekiah’s work: “he did what was good and right and faithful before the LORD his God. And every work that he undertook… he did with all his heart, and prospered” (31:20-21).`,
      [[`Malachi 3:10`, `“Bring the full tithe into the storehouse.”`], [`Nehemiah 13:10-12`, `The tithes neglected until Nehemiah restored them.`]],
      [`The leftover is a sign of blessing. How does that connect giving and abundance in your experience?`, `Hezekiah’s reform has practical results: Levites are fed. What does it show about reform that works?`],
      [`rate`, `How much do you trust that generous giving leaves a surplus?`, `Little`, `Fully`]],
    [32, `The tunnel and the test`,
      `When Sennacherib invades, Hezekiah consults his officials and blocks the springs outside the city, “Why should the kings of Assyria come and find much water?” (32:4). He strengthens the wall and “made weapons and shields in abundance” (32:5). He also “stopped the upper outlet of the waters of Gihon and directed them down to the west side of the city of David” (32:30). The Siloam Tunnel, about 533 metres long, was cut from both ends. An inscription found there in 1880 describes the moment the diggers met: “the workmen hewed… each toward his fellow… and the water flowed from the spring to the pool, 1,200 cubits.” The Chronicler records Hezekiah’s pride after his illness (32:25-26) and that God left him “to test him.”`,
      [[`2 Kings 20:20`, `The one-verse reference to the tunnel in Kings.`], [`Isaiah 22:8-11`, `Isaiah’s comment on the preparations: “you did not look to him who did it.”`]],
      [`Hezekiah prepares carefully and trusts God. What does it say about the relation of planning and trust?`, `The Siloam inscription is a rare contemporary text from the biblical era. What does it add to your reading?`],
      [`choose`, `Is preparation faithful or faithless?`, `Faithful: Hezekiah acts wisely and relies on God.`, `Ambiguous: Isaiah criticises the city for neglecting the one who planned it.`]],
    [34, `Josiah repairs the temple, and a book is found`,
      `At sixteen, Josiah begins to seek God, and at twenty he begins to purge Judah (34:3). At twenty-six he repairs the temple, and Hilkiah the priest finds “the book of the law of the LORD given through Moses” (34:14). Shaphan reads it aloud and the king tears his clothes (34:19). Huldah, a prophetess living in the Second Quarter of Jerusalem, answers: disaster will come, but because the king humbled himself, he will be gathered to his fathers in peace (34:22-28). Josiah reads the book to all the people and makes a covenant. The Chronicler places Josiah’s reform before the discovery, and the Kings account places the reform after it. Many readers think the book was some form of Deuteronomy.`,
      [[`2 Kings 22:1-20`, `The parallel account, with the discovery before the reform.`], [`Deuteronomy 28:15-68`, `Curses that might have frightened Josiah.`]],
      [`The authority is a woman, Huldah. What does her role tell you about prophecy in Judah?`, `The Chronicler puts the reform first and the book second. What does each order suggest about reform?`],
      [`choose`, `What was the book?`, `A form of Deuteronomy, as many scholars suggest.`, `A larger Torah scroll or the whole law.`]],
    [35, `Josiah’s Passover, and a death at Megiddo`,
      `Josiah keeps a Passover greater than any since Samuel (35:18). The Levites slaughter the lambs and “the priests… sprinkled the blood.” The Chronicler then adds the story of Neco of Egypt, who sends messengers: “What have I to do with you, king of Judah? I am not coming against you this day, but against the house with which I am at war… God has commanded me to hurry. Cease opposing God, who is with me, lest he destroy you” (35:21). Josiah “would not listen to the words of Neco from the mouth of God” and goes into battle disguised. Archers hit him and he dies in Jerusalem. Jeremiah chanted a lament for Josiah, and “all the singing men and singing women have spoken of Josiah in their laments to this day” (35:25).`,
      [[`2 Kings 23:29-30`, `The shorter account of Josiah’s death.`], [`Zechariah 12:11`, `“The mourning for Hadad-rimmon in the plain of Megiddo.”`]],
      [`God speaks through a foreign king in this story. What does that suggest about where God’s voice can come from?`, `Why would a good king ignore a warning? What does it say about the limits of piety?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Josiah’s death?`, `A good king who failed to listen to an unlikely messenger.`, `A tragedy that the Chronicler fits into his theme of retribution, though uneasily.`]]
  ]
};
